There are many further extensions of this work worth pursuing towards more realistic neutron star models. Such models should feature composition stratification, which is likely to have an impact on MHD equilibrium configurations (e.g. Reisenegger, 2009). Further improvements can include crustal & magnetospheric physics and studying the effects of superfluidity/superconductivity (e.g. Glampedakis, Andersson & Samuelsson, 2011).

The end-state quasi-equilibria models discovered herein add to the possible solution space of barotropic equilibria. Axisymmetric equilibrium models have been explored by Ciolfi _et al._ (2009); Ciolfi, Ferrari & Gualtieri (2010), which would be interesting to test for stability. Indeed, any pseudo-stable equilibrium could serve as possible starting points for studying the secular magnetic field evolution in magnetars and its connection to activity seen in these objects. Moreover, the theoretical modeling of magnetar QPOs (which has so far been done using symmetric magnetic field configurations), should be extended to account for the non-axisymmetric configurations akin to those encountered here. We hope to address some of these exciting issues in the near future.

